---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import NavBar from "../components/NavBar.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why not just prompts? | ShipGlows"
  description="Why ShipGlows goes beyond prompting harder."
>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <NavBar />

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why Not Just Prompts?</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">Because a better prompt cannot hold a delivery together.</h1>
        <p>
          A stronger prompt can improve one exchange. It cannot keep context,
          decisions, proof, and delivery details connected as the work moves
          forward. That is where repeated re-explanation, ambiguous scope, and
          drift between product intent, docs, code, and server operations begin.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="pricing-grid">
        <article class="price-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">What prompts can do</p>
          <h2>Make one request clearer</h2>
          <p>
            Better prompts can sharpen a single request, improve local clarity,
            and help the model produce a better immediate answer.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="price-card accent-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">What prompts do not solve</p>
          <h2>Keep the work connected after the answer</h2>
          <p>
            Prompts do not create a persistent context layer, a role-based
            documentation map, explicit work framing, completion evidence, or a
            decision-quality contract. Those mechanisms keep the fastest path
            from automatically outranking the appropriate one.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Give the next agent a usable starting point</h3>
          <p>
            ShipGlows routes work through a public métier with the relevant
            entrypoint contract and operational context. For non-trivial work,
            task contracts make scope, constraints, and proof explicit before
            the product changes.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Make completion evidence visible</h3>
          <p>
            A response or green build is not the only completion signal.
            ShipGlows prioritizes correctness, relevant security posture,
            maintainability, performance, and proof before speed, cost, or
            convenience; the checks remain proportional to the work.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Keep delivery context in view</h3>
          <p>
            Real shipping work includes environments, processes, ports,
            publishing paths, and operational checks. ShipGlows keeps that layer
            in the same frame instead of pretending the prompt is the whole job.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Tags are lighter than reprompting from scratch</h3>
          <p>
            Communicate with the AI through simple fast tags like
            <code>#offer</code>, <code>#clarity</code>, or
            <code>#shipglows</code> when you need to recenter the work without
            rewriting the whole brief.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/docs#recommended-combos">Open the focus-tags cheatsheet</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="cta-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">Short Version</p>
        <h2>ShipGlows is not anti-prompt. It is what comes after the prompt.</h2>
        <p>
          The goal is to make the whole delivery loop more reliable: context,
          execution, verification, and server operations together.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/about">Read why ShipGlows exists</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/docs">See the docs structure</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/skill-modes">Open the launch cheatsheet</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/faq">Open the FAQ</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
